\section{Why some inputs need a named function instead}{
A two-column matrix is an edgelist or a 2x2 adjacency matrix. A plain list is
a group-by-wave adjacency series, a list of graphs, or a node/edge table pair.
A data frame is an edgelist, a node table, or ordinary long data. Nothing
about those classes says which, and a converter that inspected their shape to
decide would be guessing at your data's meaning -- exactly what rnetis refuses
to do everywhere else. So each has its own function, named for what it
expects:

\describe{
\item{edgelist}{\code{\link[=netis_data_from_edgelist]{netis_data_from_edgelist()}}}
\item{node table + tie table}{\code{\link[=netis_tables]{netis_tables()}}, then this function}
\item{group-by-wave adjacency series}{\code{\link[=netis_data_from_adjacency_series]{netis_data_from_adjacency_series()}}}
\item{a single adjacency matrix, with options}{\code{\link[=netis_data_from_adjacency]{netis_data_from_adjacency()}}}
\item{an igraph graph}{\code{\link[=netis_data_from_igraph]{netis_data_from_igraph()}}, or this function}
\item{a statnet \code{network} object}{\code{\link[=netis_data_from_network]{netis_data_from_network()}}, or this function}
\item{a list of igraph graphs}{\code{\link[=netis_data_from_igraph_list]{netis_data_from_igraph_list()}}}
}

A bare \code{matrix} \strong{does} get a method, and it means an adjacency matrix. A
non-square matrix is an error naming \code{\link[=netis_data_from_edgelist]{netis_data_from_edgelist()}} rather than
being re-read as an edgelist.
}

